"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[4139],{4698(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Heuristics","permalink":"/concepts/tags/heuristics","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":5,"items":[{"id":"branch-and-bound/classic-problems","title":"Branch and Bound: Classic Problems","description":"This lesson applies the general scheme of branch and bound to several problems. In each one, the work consists of answering the same three questions:","permalink":"/concepts/branch-and-bound/classic-problems"},{"id":"branch-and-bound/exercises","title":"Exercises: Branch and Bound","description":"These exercises review the two lessons on branch and bound. They are different from the seminar activities and from the lab problems, which are left for those sessions. Try each one before reading its solution: the solutions are grouped at the end of the page.","permalink":"/concepts/branch-and-bound/exercises"},{"id":"branch-and-bound/proofs","title":"Proofs: Branch and Bound","description":"Branch and bound finds the optimal solution only if its bounds and heuristics are valid. This page proves the facts used in the lessons: why the bounds of the examples are valid, why best-first branch and bound and A\\\\* stop at the optimum, and why the puzzle heuristics and the parity condition work. Safe pruning with a bound is proved in the backtracking chapter.","permalink":"/concepts/branch-and-bound/proofs"},{"id":"branch-and-bound/branch-and-bound-scheme","title":"The Branch and Bound Scheme","description":"Branch and bound (B&B) is the last design technique of the course. Like backtracking, it searches an implicit tree of states: nodes represent states of the problem, edges represent valid changes, and solving the problem means finding a node (or a path) in that tree. The difference is the order in which the tree is explored, and that single change makes it suitable for different problems.","permalink":"/concepts/branch-and-bound/branch-and-bound-scheme"},{"id":"greedy-algorithms/greedy-method","title":"The Greedy Method","description":"Many problems ask not just for a solution, but for the best one they build the solution step by step and, at each step, they take the choice that looks best right now, without ever reconsidering it.","permalink":"/concepts/greedy-algorithms/greedy-method"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);